WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.840
(upbeat music)

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:02.490
- This episode of Otherwords was brought

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:03.960
to you by the letter R.

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:06.450
Wait, which R are we talking about?

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:08.340
The English alveolar approximant,

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:09.420
the Spanish trill,

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:10.920
the French uvular fricative?

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:13.830
Or maybe we're talking about the vowel R.

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:14.663
That's right.

00:00:14.663 --> 00:00:15.930
There's a good argument that R

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:18.240
is at least sometimes, a vowel.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:19.890
Does that mean that
your first grade teacher

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:21.000
was lying to you?

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.140
Not exactly.

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:24.120
R is an incredibly weird letter

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:26.700
with so many different
sounds and functions.

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:28.770
It's a wonder that we use one symbol

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:30.360
to represent them all.

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:32.460
And how people pronounce that one letter

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:34.410
can speak volumes about their history,

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:35.490
and social background.

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:37.470
You probably don't think much about R,

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:38.640
when you're talking,

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:42.000
but if there's one thing we
language nerds love to do

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.320
it's find the strange stories

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:46.050
beneath our ordinary
unconscious behaviors.

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:47.400
I'm Dr. Erica Brozovsky,

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:48.938
and this is Otherwords.

00:00:48.938 --> 00:00:51.521
(upbeat intro)

00:00:56.187 --> 00:00:57.354
- Other words.

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:01.410
- As a child, you were probably

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:02.460
just told to memorize

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:05.880
that the vowels were A E I O U,

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:07.290
and sometimes Y.

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:09.060
But linguists define vowels

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:12.180
not so much as letters but sounds.

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:14.460
English has around 20 vowel phonemes

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:18.150
like ee, ih, eh, aa, ay,

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:20.722
ah, aw, oh, uh,

00:01:20.722 --> 00:01:22.170
and ooh.

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:23.850
To qualify as a vowel,

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:27.090
a sound must meet a few general criteria.

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:28.950
First, it must be voiced,

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:32.070
meaning your voice box
vibrates when you make it.

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:34.710
Phonemes like ss, sh and h,

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:37.110
are voiceless, they're breath only.

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:39.660
Second, it should function as the nucleus

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:41.460
or peak of a syllable.

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:43.320
The central sound that gives a syllable

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:45.270
its length and volume.

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:46.560
It's the sound you'd sustain,

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.000
if you were singing the word.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:52.470
♪ And I-i-i ♪

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:56.400
♪ Will always love you ♪

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:58.500
And third, it must be produced

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:01.080
with an unobstructed vocal tract.

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:02.490
What the heck does that mean?

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:04.440
Your vocal tract is basically

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:07.230
the entire mechanism that shapes sounds

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:09.900
from your vocal cord to your nasal cavity.

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:12.720
The different moving parts
are known as articulators,

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:16.000
and the main ones are the
tongue, upper lip, lower lip,

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.350
upper teeth, alveolar ridge, hard palate,

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:20.970
soft palate, uvula,

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:23.040
the pharyngeal wall, and the glottis.

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:26.070
Vowel sounds are made by
contorting these articulators

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:27.330
into different shapes,

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:29.670
like pursing the lips for u,

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:31.770
or flattening the tongue for a,

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:35.310
however if any of these
articulators touch each other,

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:37.440
like pressing the tongue
against the hard palate

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:40.920
for ll, or closing your lips for mm,

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:42.810
the vocal tract is obstructed,

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:45.450
and the resulting phoneme
is considered a consonant.

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:46.950
So what's going on in your mouth

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:48.900
when you make an R sound?

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:51.180
Well, that depends on where you're from.

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:53.940
Spanish speakers will either
tap the tip of the tongue

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:56.040
to the alveolar ridge as in pero,

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:57.810
or roll it as in perro,

00:02:57.810 --> 00:02:59.310
known as a trill.

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:02.190
French speakers used to trill
their Rs with the tongue,

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:03.390
but it moved to the uvula

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:05.870
at the back of the throat, as in frere.

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:08.040
In English, the R sound is known

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:09.720
as the alveolar approximant,

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:12.360
which means the tongue
flicks backwards close

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:13.530
to the alveolar ridge,

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:15.300
but doesn't quite touch it.

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:16.740
It's a tricky move to pull off

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:18.510
and invisible from the outside,

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:20.580
which is why it's often the hardest sound

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:22.200
for young children to master.

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:23.700
What's weird is

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:25.650
that all these different sounds made

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:28.110
with different articulators
are represented

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:29.970
with just one symbol

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:30.870
and considered to be part

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:32.310
of the same phonetic family,

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:33.900
known as rhotics.

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:37.380
Interestingly, English is
one of very few languages,

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:38.910
along with Mandarin Chinese,

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:41.520
that uses the rhotic sound like a vowel,

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:45.000
In words like bird,
fur, learn, and mother,

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:47.937
the er sound is the
nucleus of the syllable.

00:03:47.937 --> 00:03:50.854
♪ I'm like a bird ♪

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:54.450
- And unlike syllabic consonants,

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:55.791
like the N in button,

00:03:55.791 --> 00:03:57.720
or the L in little,

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:00.660
there's no direct contact
between articulators,

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:03.330
leaving the vocal tract unobstructed.

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:05.580
This is why many linguists
believe that it meets

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:07.920
all the criteria of a phonetic vowel.

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:11.010
Up to now, I've only been
talking about the standard

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:13.362
American pronunciation of R,

00:04:13.362 --> 00:04:14.880
but you're probably aware

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:17.910
that not all English
speakers say it the same way.

00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:20.250
In fact, the pronunciation of R is one

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:21.930
of the most vivid differentiators

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:23.790
between English dialects.

00:04:23.790 --> 00:04:26.310
For hundreds of years, the
people of England pronounced

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:27.870
their Rs hard,

00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:29.850
similar to how it's done today in Ireland

00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:30.930
and Scotland,

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:33.000
but starting in the mid 18th century,

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:35.310
it became fashionable
to drop the rhoticity

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:36.330
at the end of a word,

00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:37.860
as in stah,

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:40.410
or before a consonant, as in paht.

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:43.410
At first, the trend was
considered lazy, and low class,

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:45.510
and many scholars railed against it.

00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:48.480
Nevertheless, it spread
throughout much of England,

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:51.030
and then exported to
colonies in South Africa,

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:52.770
Australia, and New Zealand,

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:54.600
which all now speak what are known

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:56.760
as non-rhotic English dialects.

00:04:56.760 --> 00:04:59.580
But because this trend
began after the colonization

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:00.600
of the new world,

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:02.430
most North American English speakers

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:04.826
kept their rhotic Rs, as I do today.

00:05:04.826 --> 00:05:08.160
However, some Americans
who wanted to sound proper,

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:09.960
and upper class like the British

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:12.750
did start adopting the R dropping trend.

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:14.370
It was fashionable in the US

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:16.290
up until the early 20th century,

00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:19.320
and you can hear it in
old films from the period.

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:20.463
- Don't miss a word.

00:05:21.690 --> 00:05:23.850
- Today, there are still vestiges

00:05:23.850 --> 00:05:26.100
of the dropped R in cities and regions

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:28.020
that had close contact with England,

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:30.630
like Boston, where you park your car

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:31.950
in Harvard yard,

00:05:31.950 --> 00:05:32.850
and New York,

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:35.610
where you should never
forget to wear your slicker.

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:38.310
Some New Yorkers, even
up-glided the vowel R,

00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:39.690
when it proceeded a continent,

00:05:39.690 --> 00:05:42.360
as in it's my first time at the circus.

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:45.360
The southern aristocracy
also adopted R dropping,

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:47.790
as in I do declare,

00:05:47.790 --> 00:05:50.070
which eventually spread
to most of the region,

00:05:50.070 --> 00:05:52.200
including the African-American population

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:53.400
they had enslaved,

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:56.190
and is today a feature of
African-American English.

00:05:56.190 --> 00:05:57.510
After the Civil War,

00:05:57.510 --> 00:06:00.510
as the power centers of
America shifted westward,

00:06:00.510 --> 00:06:04.050
the traditional rhotic R
became the standard once again,

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:06.930
and the dropped R, thanks
to its new association

00:06:06.930 --> 00:06:08.460
with urban areas and immigrant

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:09.630
and Black communities,

00:06:09.630 --> 00:06:12.780
reverted to a perception of
a low socioeconomic status,

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:14.130
and lack of education.

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:17.490
It's interesting that the
pronunciation of a single letter

00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:20.820
depending on the time and
place, can have such a variety

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:22.620
of social connotations.

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:25.590
It's more evidence that
there is no one correct way

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:26.623
to shape a sound.

00:06:26.623 --> 00:06:30.480
The letter R is a dramatic
symbol of how language works.

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:32.610
It's ever changing with a beautiful range

00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:34.440
of variants, and yet,

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:36.780
as long as we can understand each other,

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:37.943
it just works.

00:06:40.855 --> 00:06:41.790
Okay,

00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:42.960
why is that?

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:45.930
The er sound is the nucleus of the symbol.

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:47.338
Syllable.

00:06:47.338 --> 00:06:49.578
Ah, oh, o, eh, and oo.

00:06:49.578 --> 00:06:50.411
(laughs)

00:06:50.411 --> 00:06:51.244
Frere.

00:06:52.271 --> 00:06:53.104
Frere.

